\begin{abstract}
We study low-energy nonleptonic heavy meson decays within heavy-light chiral perturbation theory supplemented by a heavy-light chiral quark model. The framework combines heavy-quark spin-flavor symmetry with the chiral symmetry of the light degrees of freedom, allowing a systematic treatment of soft pseudoscalar mesons and \(1/m_Q\) corrections. Weak transition amplitudes are matched onto effective chiral operators, with low-energy constants estimated through the underlying quark model. We analyze the structure of factorizable and nonfactorizable contributions, including chiral loop effects and soft-gluon induced terms, and discuss their impact on decay amplitudes and strong phases. The approach provides a unified description of low-energy heavy meson transitions and offers phenomenological input for exclusive \(D\)- and \(B\)-meson decay processes.
\end{abstract}
